**What**
Every blob is written with mode `100644` and every tree with `040000`. Git
tree entries carry a mode field with several other legal values, and this
encoding uses exactly two of them. Meanwhile the dynamic reader has to
reconstruct type from structure alone, and gets it wrong in five documented
ways.

**Where**
`crates/facet-git-tree/src/ser.rs:161-165` and every other
`EntryMode::from(kind)` call site - `kind` is only ever `Blob` or `Tree`.
`crates/facet-git-tree/src/de.rs:606-650` (`deser_dynamic`) reads
`EntryKind` out of the tree and then ignores it, classifying purely on
blob-vs-tree and name shape. `docs/specification.adoc`
`deserialization.dynamic.heuristic` lists the resulting collisions.

**Why it matters**
The spec is explicit that `serialization.design.schemaless` forbids embedding a
type SHAPE, and that is a defensible position I am not arguing against. But
there is a difference between "no schema object in the tree" and "no
per-value discriminator at all", and the design currently pays the full price
of the second while only needing the first.

The five heuristic collisions all trace to one root cause - a leaf carries
bytes and nothing else, and an empty tree carries nothing at all:

| Written | Read back as |
| --- | --- |
| `Null` | empty `Object` |
| `Bool`, `Number`, `Char`, `DateTime`, `Uuid`, `QName` | `String` |
| `Bytes` that happen to be UTF-8 | `String` |
| empty `Array` | empty `Object` |
| `Object` with all-ordinal keys | `Array` |

Git offers `100755` (executable) and `120000` (symlink) as additional blob
modes, and both survive `git cat-file`, `git ls-tree`, `git diff`, and every
transport. That is roughly two spare bits per entry, at zero storage cost,
carried in a field Git already reserves for exactly this kind of
"how should this entry be interpreted" question. It is not enough to type the
whole value space, but it is enough to separate the collisions people actually
hit - for instance marking non-string scalars distinctly from strings, which
alone removes the largest row in that table.

The honest counterargument, and it is a good one: `120000` on a non-symlink
would make `git checkout` of such a tree produce garbage, and abusing mode bits
is the kind of cleverness that reads as a hack rather than a design. Worth
weighing that against the current situation, where the *documented* behaviour
is that a bare read of your data returns wrong types.

I am filing this as low severity because the schema-driven reader already
solves the problem properly, and the right answer may well be "the heuristic
read is a convenience, use a schema". But it is worth having a considered
position, because "why does a bare read of a Git-native format return the
string 4 instead of the number 4?" is a very likely audience question, and
"we chose not to spend the mode bits, here is why" is a much better answer than
"the format is schemaless".

**Options**
- Option A - use `100755` to mark non-string scalar leaves. Removes the biggest
  collision class; costs nothing on disk; `ls-tree` visibly distinguishes them.
  Trade-off: a checked-out tree would have odd executable bits, and it is
  arguably an abuse of a field with a defined meaning.
- Option B - use a filename suffix instead (`serves.n`, `title.s`). Fully
  visible in every Git tool including `diff` and `log --stat`, and unambiguous.
  Trade-off: entry names stop being clean field names, which damages the
  legibility that is the whole point.
- Option C - do nothing, and instead demote the heuristic read in the docs:
  present the schema-driven read as *the* dynamic read and the heuristic as a
  debugging aid. Trade-off: none, other than admitting the schemaless read is
  not really a feature.

**Recommendation**
Option C, medium confidence. Option A is tempting and genuinely free, but the
mode field has a meaning in Git and repurposing it undercuts the "we are
honestly Git-native, not Git-shaped" claim more than it helps. The valuable
part of this issue is having the answer ready, not necessarily changing the
encoding.
